 readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Presents the concrete windowsill plant beside the cup of water and cues learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, without revealing the target idea or naming soil, water, or sunlight as food.\",\\n        \"role\": \"orient\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Directs learners to the select-one commitment that follows, making the elicit action visible in the document sequence without authoring the interaction itself.\",\\n        \"role\": \"practice-cue\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Signals the opening orientation activity so learners know they are about to predict before being told anything.\",\\n    \"role\": \"orient\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Present the concrete case: a potted classroom plant on the windowsill, sitting next to a cup of water. Ask learners where they think the plant\\'s food comes from and have them commit to one answer before anything is explained. Do not name soil, water, or sunlight as food, and do not state the target idea that the plant makes its own food.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The objective\\'s recognition claim only becomes meaningful if the learner first holds the concrete windowsill plant and a personal guess to revise, and this is the only place the anchor can be introduced.\",\\n      \"id\": \"orient-b1\",\\n      \"intent\": \"orient\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"P