d fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"paragraph\"\\n      },\\n      \"text\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"text\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Define producer for a plant: a producer is a living thing that makes its own food. State plainly that the windowsill plant is a producer, so its food is made by the plant itself and is not something delivered to it from outside. Keep the definition to this single idea and do not yet describe how the plant makes the food or what role sunlight plays.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Directs learners to the select-one commitment that follows, making the elicit action visible in the document sequence without authoring the interaction itself.\",\\n        \"role\": \"practice-cue\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Marks the shift from guessing to the fixed category term so the definition lands as the key idea of the section.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes, holding attention on the plant as the maker while treating soil materials and water only as intakes.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Lays out the intake-versus-made distinction as a compact pair of contrasts so the separation is easy to read at a glance without introducing sunlight as food.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"States the producer definition and plainly identifies the windowsill plant as a producer whose food is made by the plant itself, staying within the single recognition-level idea.\",\\n    \"role\": \"definition\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Define producer for a plant: a producer is a living thing that makes its own food. State plainly that the windowsill plant is a producer, so its food is made by the plant itself and is not something delivered to it from outside. Keep the definition to this single idea and do not yet describe how the plant makes the food or what role sunlight plays.\",\\n    