xplain\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Opens the contrast section by naming the soil-as-food reading that will be tested against the producer idea.\",\\n        \"role\": \"contrast\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Places the soil-as-food reading beside the target on the same windowsill plant and shows why it cannot stand, since the plant still makes food inside itself.\",\\n        \"role\": \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n    \"reason\": \"Lays out the intake-versus-made distinction as a compact pair of contrasts so the separation is easy to read at a glance without introducing sunlight as food.\",\\n    \"role\": \"explain\",\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Build the idea on the same windowsill plant: the roots draw soil materials and water into the plant, but what enters through the roots is raw material the plant uses, not the plant\\'s food. The food itself is made by the plant. Hold the learner\\'s attention on the plant as the maker and treat soil and water only as intakes, so the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings are undermined without being named as errors yet. Do not introduce the sunlight-as-food idea and do not treat food and energy as interchangeable.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"Two approved misconceptions both locate food outside the plant, so this block must relocate food-making inside it by separating \\'what the plant takes in\\' from \\'what the plant makes\\' \\\\u2014 a separation a bare definition cannot make.\",\\n      \"id\": \"explain-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"explain\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nReturn JSON only 