tice-cue\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Take the windowsill plant and put the soil-as-food reading beside the target: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the soil, pulled up through the roots; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and only draws materials from the soil. Show why the soil reading cannot stand, since the plant still makes food inside itself and the soil is only one of its intakes. Do not repeat the earlier intake-versus-made distinction in full, and do not slide into the water-as-food reading here.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The slot\\'s typical intents all treat both sides as legitimate or as a slip; M1 is a held belief that must be surfaced and shut down, so an atypical intent is required and the slot purpose still matches exactly.\",\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b1\",\\n      \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Take the windowsill plant and put the soil-as-food reading beside the target: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the soil, pulled up through the roots; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and only draws materials from the soil. Show why the soil reading cannot stand, since the plant still makes food inside itself and the soil is only one of its int