elds.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"body\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      },\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"callout\"\\n      },\\n      \"title\": {\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      },\\n      \"tone\": {\\n        \"enum\": [\\n          \"note\",\\n          \"warning\",\\n          \"tip\",\\n          \"important\"\\n        ],\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"tone\",\\n      \"body\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Pose one check that asks the learner to apply the producer idea to the windowsill plant: given that the plant has soil around its roots and water poured in, decide where the plant\\'s food comes from and confirm that the plant is the thing that makes it. Keep the check to this single recognition of the plant as producer, and do not re-explain soil or water as intakes.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Signals the closing recognition check so learners know the lesson ends by applying the producer idea to the windowsill plant.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Poses the single application prompt asking where the plant\\'s food comes from and whether the plant is the maker, restricted to recognizing the plant as producer.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Points learners to the final select-one that measures whether the producer idea displaced the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings, without re-explaining soil or water as intakes.\",\\n    \"role\": \"practice-cue\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Pose one check that asks the learner to apply the producer idea to the windowsill plant: given that the plant has soil around its roots and water poured in, decide where the plant\\'s food comes from and confirm that the plant is the thing that makes it. Keep the check to this single recognition of the plant as producer, and do not re-explain soil or water as intakes.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The objective is a recognition claim about which category the plant belongs to, so the close must read whether the learner places food-making in the plant rather than in the soil or water \\\\u2014 exactly the correct-versus-incorrect signal check-understanding is for.\",\\n      \"id\": \"check-b1\",\\n      \"intent\